1
00:00:05,318 --> 00:00:10,120
It was a trial to my feelings, on the next day but one, to see Joe arraying himself in his

2
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:16,238
Sunday clothes to accompany me to Miss Havisham’s. However, as he thought his court-suit necessary

3
00:00:16,238 --> 00:00:21,398
to the occasion, it was not for me to tell him that he looked far better in his working-dress;

4
00:00:21,398 --> 00:00:25,359
the rather, because I knew he made himself so dreadfully uncomfortable,

5
00:00:25,359 --> 00:00:31,398
entirely on my account, and that it was for me he pulled up his shirt-collar so very high behind,

6
00:00:31,398 --> 00:00:36,120
that it made the hair on the crown of his head stand up like a tuft of feathers.

7
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:40,000
At breakfast-time my sister declared her intention of going to town with us,

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,719
and being left at Uncle Pumblechook’s and called for “when we had done with

9
00:00:43,719 --> 00:00:50,239
our fine ladies”—a way of putting the case, from which Joe appeared inclined to augur the worst.

10
00:00:51,439 --> 00:00:56,759
The forge was shut up for the day, and Joe inscribed in chalk upon the door (as it

11
00:00:56,760 --> 00:01:03,159
was his custom to do on the very rare occasions when he was not at work) the monosyllable HOUT,

12
00:01:03,159 --> 00:01:08,398
accompanied by a sketch of an arrow supposed to be flying in the direction he had taken.

13
00:01:08,398 --> 00:01:13,199
We walked to town, my sister leading the way in a very large beaver bonnet,

14
00:01:13,199 --> 00:01:18,279
and carrying a basket like the Great Seal of England in plaited Straw, a pair of pattens,

15
00:01:18,279 --> 00:01:23,359
a spare shawl, and an umbrella, though it was a fine bright day. I am not

16
00:01:23,359 --> 00:01:28,158
quite clear whether these articles were carried penitentially or ostentatiously;

17
00:01:28,158 --> 00:01:33,680
but I rather think they were displayed as articles of property,—much as Cleopatra or

18
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:40,359
any other sovereign lady on the Rampage might exhibit her wealth in a pageant or procession.

19
00:01:40,359 --> 00:01:47,519
When we came to Pumblechook’s, my sister bounced in and left us. As it was almost noon,

20
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:53,680
Joe and I held straight on to Miss Havisham’s house. Estella opened the gate as usual, and,

21
00:01:53,680 --> 00:02:00,079
the moment she appeared, Joe took his hat off and stood weighing it by the brim in both his hands;

22
00:02:00,079 --> 00:02:06,478
as if he had some urgent reason in his mind for being particular to half a quarter of an ounce.

23
00:02:06,478 --> 00:02:12,599
Estella took no notice of either of us, but led us the way that I knew so well. I followed next

24
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:19,478
to her, and Joe came last. When I looked back at Joe in the long passage, he was still weighing

25
00:02:19,478 --> 00:02:26,238
his hat with the greatest care, and was coming after us in long strides on the tips of his toes.

26
00:02:26,238 --> 00:02:32,120
Estella told me we were both to go in, so I took Joe by the coat-cuff and conducted him

27
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:37,158
into Miss Havisham’s presence. She was seated at her dressing-table,

28
00:02:37,158 --> 00:02:40,359
and looked round at us immediately.

29
00:02:40,359 --> 00:02:46,519
“Oh!” said she to Joe. “You are the husband of the sister of this boy?”

30
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:50,559
I could hardly have imagined dear old Joe looking so unlike himself

31
00:02:50,559 --> 00:02:56,238
or so like some extraordinary bird; standing as he did speechless,

32
00:02:56,238 --> 00:03:02,760
with his tuft of feathers ruffled, and his mouth open as if he wanted a worm.

33
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:08,799
“You are the husband,” repeated Miss Havisham, “of the sister of this boy?”

34
00:03:08,799 --> 00:03:11,920
It was very aggravating; but, throughout the interview,

35
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:16,040
Joe persisted in addressing Me instead of Miss Havisham.

36
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:21,439
“Which I meantersay, Pip,” Joe now observed in a manner that was at once expressive of forcible

37
00:03:21,439 --> 00:03:28,199
argumentation, strict confidence, and great politeness, “as I hup and married your sister,

38
00:03:28,199 --> 00:03:34,398
and I were at the time what you might call (if you was anyways inclined) a single man.”

39
00:03:34,398 --> 00:03:38,318
“Well!” said Miss Havisham. “And you have reared the boy,

40
00:03:38,318 --> 00:03:44,079
with the intention of taking him for your apprentice; is that so, Mr. Gargery?”

41
00:03:44,079 --> 00:03:48,519
“You know, Pip,” replied Joe, “as you and me were ever friends,

42
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:54,680
and it were looked for’ard to betwixt us, as being calc’lated to lead to larks. Not but what,

43
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:59,840
Pip, if you had ever made objections to the business,—such as its being open to black and sut,

44
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:05,120
or such-like,—not but what they would have been attended to, don’t you see?”

45
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:12,158
“Has the boy,” said Miss Havisham, “ever made any objection? Does he like the trade?”

46
00:04:12,158 --> 00:04:17,920
“Which it is well beknown to yourself, Pip,” returned Joe, strengthening his former mixture of

47
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:24,760
argumentation, confidence, and politeness, “that it were the wish of your own hart.” (I saw the

48
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:29,398
idea suddenly break upon him that he would adapt his epitaph to the occasion, before he went on to

49
00:04:29,398 --> 00:04:36,718
say) “And there weren’t no objection on your part, and Pip it were the great wish of your hart!”

50
00:04:36,719 --> 00:04:40,318
It was quite in vain for me to endeavour to make him sensible that he ought to speak

51
00:04:40,318 --> 00:04:45,120
to Miss Havisham. The more I made faces and gestures to him to do it,

52
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:50,040
the more confidential, argumentative, and polite, he persisted in being to Me.

53
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:55,000
“Have you brought his indentures with you?” asked Miss Havisham.

54
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,318
“Well, Pip, you know,” replied Joe, as if that were a little unreasonable, “you yourself see me

55
00:05:01,318 --> 00:05:07,679
put ’em in my ’at, and therefore you know as they are here.” With which he took them out, and gave

56
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:15,040
them, not to Miss Havisham, but to me. I am afraid I was ashamed of the dear good fellow,—I know I

57
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:20,760
was ashamed of him,—when I saw that Estella stood at the back of Miss Havisham’s chair, and that her

58
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:28,840
eyes laughed mischievously. I took the indentures out of his hand and gave them to Miss Havisham.

59
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:35,079
“You expected,” said Miss Havisham, as she looked them over, “no premium with the boy?”

60
00:05:35,079 --> 00:05:41,120
“Joe!” I remonstrated, for he made no reply at all. “Why don’t you answer—”

61
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:45,639
“Pip,” returned Joe, cutting me short as if he were hurt, “which I meantersay

62
00:05:45,639 --> 00:05:50,079
that were not a question requiring a answer betwixt yourself and me, and which you know

63
00:05:50,079 --> 00:05:56,238
the answer to be full well No. You know it to be No, Pip, and wherefore should I say it?”

64
00:05:56,238 --> 00:06:00,760
Miss Havisham glanced at him as if she understood what he really was better than I had thought

65
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:07,439
possible, seeing what he was there; and took up a little bag from the table beside her.

66
00:06:07,439 --> 00:06:10,959
“Pip has earned a premium here,” she said, “and here it is. There

67
00:06:10,959 --> 00:06:15,719
are five-and-twenty guineas in this bag. Give it to your master, Pip.”

68
00:06:15,719 --> 00:06:19,158
As if he were absolutely out of his mind with the wonder awakened in him

69
00:06:19,158 --> 00:06:26,279
by her strange figure and the strange room, Joe, even at this pass, persisted in addressing me.

70
00:06:26,279 --> 00:06:32,279
“This is wery liberal on your part, Pip,” said Joe, “and it is as such received and grateful

71
00:06:32,279 --> 00:06:39,519
welcome, though never looked for, far nor near, nor nowheres. And now, old chap,” said Joe,

72
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:44,120
conveying to me a sensation, first of burning and then of freezing, for I felt as

73
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:49,840
if that familiar expression were applied to Miss Havisham,—“and now, old chap, may we do our duty!

74
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:55,439
May you and me do our duty, both on us, by one and another, and by them

75
00:06:55,439 --> 00:07:02,040
which your liberal present—have-conweyed—to be—for the satisfaction of mind-of—them as

76
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:07,760
never—” here Joe showed that he felt he had fallen into frightful difficulties,

77
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:14,120
until he triumphantly rescued himself with the words, “and from myself far be it!” These

78
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:19,238
words had such a round and convincing sound for him that he said them twice.

79
00:07:19,238 --> 00:07:24,359
“Good-bye, Pip!” said Miss Havisham. “Let them out, Estella.”

80
00:07:24,359 --> 00:07:27,318
“Am I to come again, Miss Havisham?” I asked.

81
00:07:27,318 --> 00:07:33,000
“No. Gargery is your master now. Gargery! One word!”

82
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,719
Thus calling him back as I went out of the door,

83
00:07:35,719 --> 00:07:42,359
I heard her say to Joe in a distinct emphatic voice, “The boy has been a good boy here,

84
00:07:42,359 --> 00:07:49,838
and that is his reward. Of course, as an honest man, you will expect no other and no more.”

85
00:07:50,398 --> 00:07:55,478
How Joe got out of the room, I have never been able to determine; but I know that when he did get

86
00:07:55,478 --> 00:08:01,519
out he was steadily proceeding upstairs instead of coming down, and was deaf to all remonstrances

87
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:07,680
until I went after him and laid hold of him. In another minute we were outside the gate,

88
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:14,318
and it was locked, and Estella was gone. When we stood in the daylight alone again,

89
00:08:14,318 --> 00:08:19,959
Joe backed up against a wall, and said to me, “Astonishing!” And there he remained so

90
00:08:19,959 --> 00:08:24,959
long saying, “Astonishing” at intervals, so often, that I began to think his senses were

91
00:08:24,959 --> 00:08:30,520
never coming back. At length he prolonged his remark into “Pip, I do assure you

92
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:38,318
this is as-TON-ishing!” and so, by degrees, became conversational and able to walk away.

93
00:08:38,318 --> 00:08:42,199
I have reason to think that Joe’s intellects were brightened by the encounter they had

94
00:08:42,199 --> 00:08:46,478
passed through, and that on our way to Pumblechook’s he invented a subtle and

95
00:08:46,478 --> 00:08:52,918
deep design. My reason is to be found in what took place in Mr. Pumblechook’s parlour: where,

96
00:08:52,920 --> 00:08:58,520
on our presenting ourselves, my sister sat in conference with that detested seedsman.

97
00:08:58,520 --> 00:09:02,920
“Well?” cried my sister, addressing us both at once. “And what’s happened to

98
00:09:02,920 --> 00:09:09,959
you? I wonder you condescend to come back to such poor society as this, I am sure I do!”

99
00:09:09,959 --> 00:09:15,359
“Miss Havisham,” said Joe, with a fixed look at me, like an effort of remembrance,

100
00:09:15,359 --> 00:09:19,718
“made it wery partick’ler that we should give her—were it compliments or respects, Pip?”

101
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:22,398
“Compliments,” I said.

102
00:09:22,398 --> 00:09:28,478
“Which that were my own belief,” answered Joe; “her compliments to Mrs. J. Gargery—”

103
00:09:28,478 --> 00:09:34,279
“Much good they’ll do me!” observed my sister; but rather gratified too.

104
00:09:34,279 --> 00:09:40,199
“And wishing,” pursued Joe, with another fixed look at me, like another effort of remembrance,

105
00:09:40,199 --> 00:09:46,638
“that the state of Miss Havisham’s elth were sitch as would have—allowed, were it, Pip?”

106
00:09:46,639 --> 00:09:49,318
“Of her having the pleasure,” I added.

107
00:09:49,318 --> 00:09:54,519
“Of ladies’ company,” said Joe. And drew a long breath.

108
00:09:54,520 --> 00:10:00,120
“Well!” cried my sister, with a mollified glance at Mr. Pumblechook. “She might have

109
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:02,519
had the politeness to send that message at first,

110
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:08,238
but it’s better late than never. And what did she give young Rantipole here?”

111
00:10:08,238 --> 00:10:11,359
“She giv’ him,” said Joe, “nothing.”

112
00:10:11,359 --> 00:10:15,359
Mrs. Joe was going to break out, but Joe went on.

113
00:10:15,359 --> 00:10:19,838
“What she giv’,” said Joe, “she giv’ to his friends. ‘And by

114
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:24,439
his friends,’ were her explanation, ‘I mean into the hands of his sister Mrs.

115
00:10:24,439 --> 00:10:32,359
J. Gargery.’ Them were her words; ‘Mrs. J. Gargery.’ She mayn’t have know’d,” added Joe,

116
00:10:32,359 --> 00:10:36,959
with an appearance of reflection, “whether it were Joe, or Jorge.”

117
00:10:36,959 --> 00:10:41,959
My sister looked at Pumblechook: who smoothed the elbows of his wooden arm-chair,

118
00:10:41,959 --> 00:10:47,279
and nodded at her and at the fire, as if he had known all about it beforehand.

119
00:10:47,279 --> 00:10:52,679
“And how much have you got?” asked my sister, laughing. Positively laughing!

120
00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:56,799
“What would present company say to ten pound?” demanded Joe.

121
00:10:56,799 --> 00:11:02,719
“They’d say,” returned my sister, curtly, “pretty well. Not too much, but pretty well.”

122
00:11:02,719 --> 00:11:08,318
“It’s more than that, then,” said Joe. That fearful Impostor, Pumblechook,

123
00:11:08,318 --> 00:11:15,279
immediately nodded, and said, as he rubbed the arms of his chair, “It’s more than that, Mum.”

124
00:11:15,279 --> 00:11:18,318
“Why, you don’t mean to say—” began my sister.

125
00:11:18,318 --> 00:11:23,920
“Yes I do, Mum,” said Pumblechook; “but wait a bit. Go on, Joseph. Good in you! Go on!”

126
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:28,040
“What would present company say,” proceeded Joe, “to twenty pound?”

127
00:11:28,040 --> 00:11:31,879
“Handsome would be the word,” returned my sister.

128
00:11:31,879 --> 00:11:35,799
“Well, then,” said Joe, “It’s more than twenty pound.”

129
00:11:35,799 --> 00:11:41,520
That abject hypocrite, Pumblechook, nodded again, and said, with a patronizing laugh,

130
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:46,398
“It’s more than that, Mum. Good again! Follow her up, Joseph!”

131
00:11:46,398 --> 00:11:48,558
“Then to make an end of it,” said Joe,

132
00:11:48,559 --> 00:11:53,520
delightedly handing the bag to my sister; “it’s five-and-twenty pound.”

133
00:11:53,520 --> 00:11:58,920
“It’s five-and-twenty pound, Mum,” echoed that basest of swindlers, Pumblechook,

134
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:03,559
rising to shake hands with her; “and it’s no more than your merits (as I

135
00:12:03,559 --> 00:12:08,279
said when my opinion was asked), and I wish you joy of the money!”

136
00:12:08,279 --> 00:12:12,599
If the villain had stopped here, his case would have been sufficiently awful,

137
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:16,478
but he blackened his guilt by proceeding to take me into custody,

138
00:12:16,478 --> 00:12:21,359
with a right of patronage that left all his former criminality far behind.

139
00:12:21,359 --> 00:12:27,158
“Now you see, Joseph and wife,” said Pumblechook, as he took me by the arm above the elbow,

140
00:12:27,158 --> 00:12:33,120
“I am one of them that always go right through with what they’ve begun. This boy must be bound,

141
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:38,439
out of hand. That’s my way. Bound out of hand.”

142
00:12:38,439 --> 00:12:42,318
“Goodness knows, Uncle Pumblechook,” said my sister (grasping the money),

143
00:12:42,318 --> 00:12:45,000
“we’re deeply beholden to you.”

144
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,959
“Never mind me, Mum,” returned that diabolical cornchandler.

145
00:12:47,959 --> 00:12:52,959
“A pleasure’s a pleasure all the world over. But this boy, you know;

146
00:12:52,959 --> 00:12:58,079
we must have him bound. I said I’d see to it—to tell you the truth.”

147
00:12:58,079 --> 00:13:02,318
The Justices were sitting in the Town Hall near at hand, and we at once went over to

148
00:13:02,318 --> 00:13:08,398
have me bound apprentice to Joe in the Magisterial presence. I say we went over,

149
00:13:08,398 --> 00:13:13,679
but I was pushed over by Pumblechook, exactly as if I had that moment picked a pocket or fired a

150
00:13:13,680 --> 00:13:19,600
rick; indeed, it was the general impression in Court that I had been taken red-handed; for,

151
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:24,680
as Pumblechook shoved me before him through the crowd, I heard some people say, “What’s

152
00:13:24,680 --> 00:13:31,959
he done?” and others, “He’s a young ’un, too, but looks bad, don’t he?” One person of mild

153
00:13:31,959 --> 00:13:36,719
and benevolent aspect even gave me a tract ornamented with a woodcut of a malevolent

154
00:13:36,719 --> 00:13:43,520
young man fitted up with a perfect sausage-shop of fetters, and entitled TO BE READ IN MY CELL.

155
00:13:43,520 --> 00:13:48,559
The Hall was a queer place, I thought, with higher pews in it than a church,—and with

156
00:13:48,559 --> 00:13:54,238
people hanging over the pews looking on,—and with mighty Justices (one with a powdered head)

157
00:13:54,238 --> 00:14:00,519
leaning back in chairs, with folded arms, or taking snuff, or going to sleep, or writing,

158
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:07,439
or reading the newspapers,—and with some shining black portraits on the walls, which my unartistic

159
00:14:07,439 --> 00:14:14,879
eye regarded as a composition of hardbake and sticking-plaster. Here, in a corner my indentures

160
00:14:14,879 --> 00:14:21,158
were duly signed and attested, and I was “bound”; Mr. Pumblechook holding me all the while as if we

161
00:14:21,158 --> 00:14:26,638
had looked in on our way to the scaffold, to have those little preliminaries disposed of.

162
00:14:26,639 --> 00:14:30,079
When we had come out again, and had got rid of the boys who had been put into

163
00:14:30,079 --> 00:14:34,120
great spirits by the expectation of seeing me publicly tortured,

164
00:14:34,120 --> 00:14:38,719
and who were much disappointed to find that my friends were merely rallying round me,

165
00:14:38,719 --> 00:14:45,559
we went back to Pumblechook’s. And there my sister became so excited by the twenty-five guineas,

166
00:14:45,559 --> 00:14:49,319
that nothing would serve her but we must have a dinner out of that windfall at the Blue Boar,

167
00:14:49,879 --> 00:14:56,478
and that Pumblechook must go over in his chaise-cart, and bring the Hubbles and Mr. Wopsle.

168
00:14:56,478 --> 00:15:03,158
It was agreed to be done; and a most melancholy day I passed. For, it inscrutably appeared to

169
00:15:03,158 --> 00:15:07,840
stand to reason, in the minds of the whole company, that I was an excrescence on the

170
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:14,238
entertainment. And to make it worse, they all asked me from time to time,—in short,

171
00:15:14,238 --> 00:15:19,040
whenever they had nothing else to do,—why I didn’t enjoy myself? And

172
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:25,398
what could I possibly do then, but say I was enjoying myself,—when I wasn’t!

173
00:15:25,398 --> 00:15:29,879
However, they were grown up and had their own way, and they made the most of it.

174
00:15:29,879 --> 00:15:35,840
That swindling Pumblechook, exalted into the beneficent contriver of the whole occasion,

175
00:15:35,840 --> 00:15:41,719
actually took the top of the table; and, when he addressed them on the subject of my being bound,

176
00:15:41,719 --> 00:15:49,435
and had fiendishly congratulated them on my being liable to imprisonment if I played at cards,

177
00:15:49,435 --> 00:15:53,079
drank strong liquors, kept late hours or bad company, or indulged in other vagaries which

178
00:15:53,079 --> 00:15:58,639
the form of my indentures appeared to contemplate as next to inevitable, he placed me standing on

179
00:15:58,639 --> 00:16:05,040
a chair beside him to illustrate his remarks. My only other remembrances of the great festival

180
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:11,158
are, That they wouldn’t let me go to sleep, but whenever they saw me dropping off, woke me up

181
00:16:11,158 --> 00:16:19,318
and told me to enjoy myself. That, rather late in the evening Mr. Wopsle gave us Collins’s ode, and

182
00:16:19,318 --> 00:16:24,879
threw his bloodstained sword in thunder down, with such effect, that a waiter came in and said, “The

183
00:16:24,879 --> 00:16:30,520
Commercials underneath sent up their compliments, and it wasn’t the Tumblers’ Arms.” That, they were

184
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:37,439
all in excellent spirits on the road home, and sang, O Lady Fair! Mr. Wopsle taking the bass,

185
00:16:37,439 --> 00:16:43,478
and asserting with a tremendously strong voice (in reply to the inquisitive bore who leads that piece

186
00:16:43,478 --> 00:16:49,238
of music in a most impertinent manner, by wanting to know all about everybody’s private affairs)

187
00:16:50,199 --> 00:16:53,519
that he was the man with his white locks flowing, and that

188
00:16:53,520 --> 00:17:00,680
he was upon the whole the weakest pilgrim going. Finally, I remember that when I got into my little

189
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:06,158
bedroom, I was truly wretched, and had a strong conviction on me that I should never like Joe’s

190
00:17:06,158 --> 00:17:12,000
trade. I had liked it once, but once was not now. Chapter 14.
